### What makes a technical decision "tough"?

A decision is tough when there are competing constraints : time versus correctness, simplicity versus scalability, speed versus safety. In a banking context at National Bank of India, the classic tension is between strong consistency and higher availability for Rajesh's and Priya's fund transfers. Both options are defensible, and each has a real cost.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class DecisionTension {
    enum Priority { CORRECTNESS, AVAILABILITY, SPEED, COST }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // The decision is tough because these compete
        System.out.println("Option A optimizes: " + Priority.CORRECTNESS + " + simplicity");
        System.out.println("Option B optimizes: " + Priority.AVAILABILITY + " + " + Priority.SPEED);
        System.out.println("You cannot have both without a trade-off.");
    }
}
```

### How do you structure the STAR for this question?

Situation: the context and what made the decision hard. Task: your specific responsibility in making the call. Action: the options you identified, the criteria you scored them on, and how you decided. Result: the measurable outcome and what you learned.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class ToughDecisionSTAR {
    record STAR(String situation, String task, String action, String result) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        STAR story = new STAR(
            "Transfer service needed redesign: sync vs async architecture",
            "I owned the architecture decision for the money-movement path",
            "Scored both options on correctness, latency, resilience, cost",
            "Chose async: failure rate dropped, system stayed up during dependency wobbles"
        );
        System.out.println(story);
    }
}
```

### How do you show a structured decision method?

Senior engineers decide with data and criteria, not gut feeling alone. Show that you scored options against weighted criteria. For a banking transfer service, correctness weighs the heaviest because money must balance exactly.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.Map;

public class WeightedDecision {
    public static BigDecimal score(Map<String, BigDecimal> weights, Map<String, BigDecimal> ratings) {
        return weights.entrySet().stream()
            .map(e -> e.getValue().multiply(ratings.getOrDefault(e.getKey(), BigDecimal.ZERO)))
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add)
            .setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, BigDecimal> weights = Map.of(
            "correctness", new BigDecimal("0.50"),
            "latency",     new BigDecimal("0.30"),
            "cost",        new BigDecimal("0.20")
        );
        Map<String, BigDecimal> syncRatings  = Map.of("correctness", new BigDecimal("9"), "latency", new BigDecimal("5"), "cost", new BigDecimal("8"));
        Map<String, BigDecimal> asyncRatings = Map.of("correctness", new BigDecimal("8"), "latency", new BigDecimal("9"), "cost", new BigDecimal("6"));

        System.out.println("Sync score:  " + score(weights, syncRatings));
        System.out.println("Async score: " + score(weights, asyncRatings));
    }
}
```

```text
Sync score:  7.60
Async score: 7.90
```

### What are the two real options in a banking transfer design?

Option A is synchronous and strongly consistent: debit and credit in a single database transaction. Simple and absolutely correct, but couples availability. Option B is asynchronous and eventually consistent using events: decouples services but requires reconciliation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

public class SyncTransfer {
    // Option A: simple, correct, but availability-coupled
    @Transactional
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        debit(fromId, amount);
        credit(toId, amount);
    }

    private void debit(String id, java.math.BigDecimal amt)  { /* ... */ }
    private void credit(String id, java.math.BigDecimal amt) { /* ... */ }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class AsyncTransfer {
    // Option B: resilient, fast, but needs reconciliation
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        String requestId = java.util.UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        publishEvent(new TransferRequested(requestId, fromId, toId, amount));
    }

    private void publishEvent(Object event) { /* Kafka publish */ }
    record TransferRequested(String requestId, String from, String to, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {}
}
```

### How do you acknowledge the trade-off?

Every real decision has a cost. Pretending otherwise signals shallow thinking. Name the downside of your choice and explain how you mitigated it.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class TradeOffLog {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(TradeOffLog.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String chosen = "Asynchronous event-driven design";
        String tradeOff = "Requires reconciliation for stuck transfers";
        String mitigation = "Built idempotent reconciliation job running every 5 minutes";
        log.info(() -> "Chose: " + chosen + " | Trade-off: " + tradeOff + " | Mitigation: " + mitigation);
    }
}
```

### How do you involve the right people?

Technical decisions in a team are social as well as technical. Walk the team and the operations side through the scoring so the decision is shared, not imposed.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.List;

public class DecisionCommunication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> stakeholders = List.of("Team lead", "Ops engineer", "Product owner");
        stakeholders.forEach(s -> System.out.println("Shared scoring with: " + s));
        System.out.println("Decision was collective, not imposed.");
    }
}
```

### What is the difference between one-way and two-way door decisions?

A one-way door decision is hard to reverse: choosing a database engine, a public API contract. A two-way door decision is easy to reverse: a feature flag, an internal library choice. You fight harder and gather more data on one-way doors; you move fast on two-way doors.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class DoorType {
    enum Door { ONE_WAY, TWO_WAY }

    public static Door classify(boolean easilyReversible) {
        return easilyReversible ? Door.TWO_WAY : Door.ONE_WAY;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Database choice: " + classify(false));
        System.out.println("Internal library: " + classify(true));
    }
}
```

```text
Database choice: ONE_WAY
Internal library: TWO_WAY
```

### What should a senior engineer emphasize in the result?

Close with the measurable outcome, a reflection on whether the decision held up over time, and what you would do differently. This signals continuous learning.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class DecisionResult {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(DecisionResult.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Result: transfer system stayed up during dependency wobbles");
        log.info("Failure rate dropped from 3% to under 0.5%");
        log.info("Reflection: I would prototype earlier next time to get numbers faster");
    }
}
```
